    "description": "The Cradley Deluxe is the can we would point most gardeners to first. Haws rates it at one and a half gallons, or 7 litres, which is about 15 pounds of water when full. That is a real working capacity for a patio, a run of containers or a small vegetable bed, but still light enough to carry level. The key upgrade over cheaper plastic cans is the fittings. Haws equips it with a detachable, high-flow oval brass rose and a brass ferrule, which means you can fit the rose face up for seedlings and swap it for any head in the Haws all-brass range later. The long reach spout, listed at 64 cm overall, keeps flow steady and lets you water the back of a bed without leaning in. The body is recyclable plastic, so there is nothing to rust and nothing to chip. It costs much more than a big-box plastic can, but the rose alone is the difference between a gentle shower and a splashy stream. Note the cheaper Cradley Cascader uses a plastic ferrule and a brass-faced rose, so check which one you are buying."
