          <a href="#what-not-to-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. What Not to Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The short answer: the Haws Cradley Deluxe is the best watering can for most gardens, because 7 litres is a weight you can actually carry and its brass rose waters gently enough for seedlings. The Bloem Classic is the budget pick, the Behrens 210 is the galvanized workhorse, and indoor plants want a small long-spout can like the Haws Bartley Burbler or Burgon &amp; Ball Indoor, not an outdoor can.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="capacity-vs-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Capacity vs Weight: The 8.3-Pound Rule
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every watering can decision starts with one number: a US gallon of water weighs about 8.3 pounds. A 1-gallon can is roughly 8 to 9 pounds full once you add the can itself. A 2-gallon can is 17 pounds of water before the steel or plastic. A 2.5-gallon can like the Behrens 210 carries close to 21 pounds of water. That is the difference between an easy lap of the patio pots and a strained wrist by the third refill.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What a full can actually weighs (water only)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2.2 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1 litre indoor can</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8.3 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1 gallon</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">16.7 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">2 gallons</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20.9 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">2.5 gallons</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Bigger is not automatically better. A can is only efficient if you fill it to the top, and most people stop filling a 2.5-gallon can at the point where it gets too heavy to carry level. If you routinely fill a big can halfway, you bought the wrong can. Buy the size you will actually fill, carry and pour without the spout swinging around.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other half of the weight question is distance. Carrying 17 pounds ten feet from a spigot to a raised bed is fine for most adults. Carrying it 80 feet to the back fence, six times, is a job for a hose. Cans earn their place for seedlings, containers, liquid feed and the corners of the yard where a hose kinks or drags across plants.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide: 1 to 2 litres for houseplants, 1 to 1.5 gallons for a balcony or patio of containers, and 2 to 2.5 gallons only if you are strong, the walk is short and you water beds rather than pots. Most households with a mix of indoor and outdoor plants end up best served by two cans, not one huge one.
          </p>
        </div>

