            We did not run a watering can lab, and we are not going to pretend we did. These picks are built from maker specifications, retailer spec sheets, published dimensions and capacities, rose and ferrule compatibility notes from the manufacturers, and recurring patterns in owner reports. Where sources disagree, as they do on several Haws and Burgon &amp; Ball dimensions, we say so or leave the number out.
          </p>

          <p>
            We filtered on five things. Capacity had to be stated by the maker, not guessed. The rose had to be described clearly: removable or fixed, brass or plastic. Material had to be specific enough to predict rust behaviour, since galvanized, powder-coated and plain painted steel age very differently. The can had to be a current model you can still buy. And each pick had to do a distinct job, so you are not choosing between six versions of the same green plastic can.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out decorative cans whose spout or rose design does not deliver usable flow, and novelty shapes with no published capacity. We also avoided ranking anything on star ratings, which we cannot verify and which mostly measure shipping and colour accuracy rather than pouring.
          </p>
        </div>

