        <div id="rose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Rose: Brass vs Plastic, Face Up vs Face Down
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rose is the perforated head on the end of the spout, and it is the part that separates a good can from a bad one. A good rose breaks the stream into many fine jets so water lands softly and soaks in rather than blasting soil off the roots. A poor rose dribbles from the face, sprays sideways from a loose fit or clogs after one season of hard water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brass roses are the premium choice for a reason. Brass holds a crisp, consistent hole size, resists cracking in frost and can be cleaned with a pin without deforming. Haws fits an oval brass rose to the Cradley Deluxe and the Warley Fall, and its brass ferrule (the collar the rose slides onto) accepts the full Haws all-brass rose range. Plastic roses work, and the Bloem cans use them, but the holes are larger, the spray coarser and the fit looser over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brass-faced roses sit between the two: a brass plate with the holes, on a plastic body. Haws uses this on cheaper cans such as the Cradley Cascader and the Bartley Burbler. You get most of the spray quality for less money, though not the full-brass durability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why gardeners turn the rose upward</h3>

          <p>
            On a traditional can with a removable rose, you can fit it with the face pointing up rather than forward. Water then rises in a gentle arc and falls back down like light rain, which is the standard way to water seedlings and freshly sown trays without washing seed out of the compost. Face forward gives a heavier, more directional shower for established plants and beds. A can with a fixed rose, like the Behrens 210, cannot do this, which is the main reason we do not recommend it for seed trays.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spout-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Spout Length: Indoor vs Outdoor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spout length decides where water goes, and it matters more indoors than out. Houseplants want a long, narrow spout and no rose at all, so you can reach under leaves and deliver water to the soil surface without wetting foliage or splashing the windowsill. Small indoor cans in the 0.7 to 1 litre range, like the Burgon &amp; Ball and the Bartley Burbler, are built around that reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoor cans need a long spout for a different reason: leverage and flow control. A long-reach spout lets you water the back of a border or a raised bed without leaning over it, and it keeps the outlet below the waterline of the can so you get a steady stream instead of glugging. The Haws Cradley Deluxe is listed at 64 cm long overall, much of it spout, which is why it pours so evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Short, stubby spouts on cheap cans are the most common flaw. They pour fast and splash, and they force you to tilt the whole can steeply, which shifts the weight forward onto your wrist. If you water hanging baskets or tall containers, a long stem spout like the Bloem Aqua Rite's is worth far more than extra capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule of thumb: no rose and a thin spout for houseplants; long spout plus removable rose for seedlings and beds; long stem spout with a small outlet for hanging baskets and pots on stands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Galvanized vs Plastic vs Powder-Coated Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plastic cans are light, cheap, quiet and do not rust. The Bloem Classic weighs very little empty, so almost all the weight you carry is water. The downsides are UV brittleness over years in full sun, coarser plastic roses, and a tendency for thin handles to flex when the can is full. Good plastic cans, like the Haws Cradley range, use thicker walls and better ferrules, and they are the sensible default for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot-dip galvanized steel, as on the Behrens 210 and the Warley Fall HDG, is dipped in molten zinc after it is formed, so seams and edges get coated too. That is the most rust-resistant way to make a steel can and the reason these cans last decades. Pre-galvanized steel, made from sheet that is zinc-coated before forming, is still durable but leaves cut edges less protected. Galvanized cans are heavier empty and ring loudly when you fill them, which some people love and some people hate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder-coated steel is what most small indoor metal cans use, including the Burgon &amp; Ball. It looks good and resists rust well, but the coating can chip, and at least one retailer warns that joints such as where the spout meets the body may still rust over time. Treat a powder-coated indoor can as a houseplant tool, not something to leave in the garden over winter.
          </p>

