        <div id="balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Balance, Handles and How a Can Pours
          </h2>

          <p>
            A can that weighs the same can feel completely different in the hand. The best designs put a long top handle over the centre of mass for carrying and a rear handle low down for pouring, so you can tip the can with your wrist straight. Haws cans and the Behrens 210 both use this two-handle layout. Single loop handles, common on cheap plastic cans, make you lift and tilt at the same time, which is where the wrist strain comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at where the spout leaves the body. A spout that starts near the base keeps the outlet low, gives steady flow from full to almost empty, and lets you drain the can completely. A spout that starts high up means you have to tip the can almost upside down to get the last litre out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fill opening matters too. A wide opening fills quickly under an outdoor tap and lets you rinse out algae and fertilizer residue. A narrow one fills slowly and splashes. Very large openings on cheap cans slosh when you walk, which is why many traditional cans have a partly covered top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Size Watering Can Do You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the can to the plants, not to the biggest number on the shelf. Houseplants rarely need more than a litre at a time, and a big outdoor can is clumsy indoors and will drip from the rose onto floors. Container gardens and balconies are the sweet spot for 1 to 1.5 gallon cans. Vegetable beds and borders far from a tap are where 2 gallons start to pay off, provided you can carry them.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
