          </div>

          <p>
            If you only buy one can for a mixed indoor and outdoor collection, a 1 to 1.5 gallon outdoor can with a removable rose is the best compromise: pull the rose off indoors for a direct stream, put it back on outside. It will still be too big for a crowded windowsill, which is why so many plant owners end up adding a small indoor can later.
          </p>

          <p>
            For anyone who struggles with weight, there is no shame in going smaller and making more trips. Two trips with 1 gallon is about 8 pounds each time, and that is far kinder on shoulders than one trip with 17.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Making a Watering Can Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most watering cans die from neglect, not wear. Water left standing in a can grows algae, leaves mineral scale in the rose and, in metal cans, sits against seams for months. Empty the can after use and store it upside down or on its side so it dries. That one habit adds years to any can, plastic or steel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frost is the big killer. Water expanding as it freezes splits plastic seams and can pop the solder or crimping on metal cans. Retailers selling the Haws Warley Fall specifically advise against leaving it outside over winter with water inside. Bring cans into a shed or garage once nights drop below freezing, or at minimum leave them empty and inverted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fertilizer is the second. Liquid feeds are often acidic or salty, and leaving them in a galvanized can attacks the zinc coating. Some Haws retailers advise against using plant food in the Warley Fall at all. If you feed regularly, keep a cheap plastic can for fertilizer and your good metal can for plain water, and rinse both after every use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean a clogged rose by soaking it in white vinegar to soften scale, then clearing the holes with a pin. Brass roses tolerate this indefinitely. Plastic roses can be cleaned the same way but tend to crack at the edges after a few years, and replacements are not always sold separately, so check before you buy if spare parts matter to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When a Can Is the Wrong Tool, and What Not to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            A watering can is a precision tool, not a lawn irrigation system. If you are filling a can more than five or six times per session, a hose will save your back and your evening. A decent <a href="/best-garden-hose/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">garden hose</a> with a good <a href="/best-garden-hose-nozzle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hose nozzle</a> set to shower does most of what a large can does across beds, and a lightweight <a href="/best-expandable-garden-hose/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">expandable hose</a> is easier to move around a small patio than a 2-gallon can is to carry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip decorative cans with no rose, tiny spouts and no stated capacity. They pour in a heavy stream that washes soil out of pots. Skip very cheap thin-walled plastic cans with a single loop handle in the 2.5 gallon and larger sizes: the handle flexes, the water sloshes and the rose falls off. And skip any metal can sold only as steel or metal with no mention of galvanizing or powder coating, since plain painted steel will rust from the inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where cans do shine is in new plantings, seed trays, containers and the feed and care jobs that pair naturally with the rest of your <a href="/best-garden-tools/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">garden tools</a>. If you are planning new beds or containers, our <a href="/diy-garden-landscaping-ideas/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">DIY garden landscaping ideas</a> guide covers how to lay out a space so water, by can or hose, actually reaches everything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Biggest Can You Can Find</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2.5 gallon can holds about 21 pounds of water. Most people stop filling it halfway because it is too heavy, which wastes the capacity and still strains the wrist. Buy the size you will actually fill and carry level: often 1 to 1.5 gallons outdoors and 1 litre indoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Fixed or Coarse Rose on Seedlings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy stream washes seed out of trays and knocks over young seedlings. Use a fine rose fitted face up, so water arcs upward and falls like rain. Fixed-rose cans and cans with no rose at all are the wrong tool for anything freshly sown.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Water in the Can Over Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water expanding as it freezes splits plastic seams and damages metal cans. Empty cans after use, store them upside down or on their side, and bring them into a shed or garage once nights fall below freezing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Fertilizer in a Galvanized Can</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Liquid feeds can be acidic or salty and attack the zinc coating that keeps a galvanized can from rusting. Keep a cheap plastic can for fertilizer, use your metal can for plain water only, and rinse both after every use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Outdoor Can on Houseplants</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big outdoor can with a rose drips onto floors, sprays leaves and is awkward to aim on a shelf. Houseplants need a small can with a long, thin spout. Either buy a dedicated indoor can or pull the rose off and pour slowly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

