          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best watering can?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most gardens, the Haws Cradley Deluxe is the best all-round can: 7 litres is a manageable full weight, and its brass rose and brass ferrule give a fine spray and long-term rose compatibility. On a budget, the Bloem Classic 2 gallon is the best value. Indoors, the Haws Bartley Burbler is the most versatile small can.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size watering can should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 1 litre for houseplants, about 1 to 1.5 gallons for containers and patios, and 2 to 2.5 gallons only for beds and borders close to a tap. Remember each gallon adds about 8.3 pounds. If in doubt, go smaller: two light trips are easier than one heavy one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are metal or plastic watering cans better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plastic is lighter, cheaper and cannot rust, which makes it the better default. Hot-dip galvanized steel lasts longer and resists UV, but it is heavier and should never hold fertilizer. Powder-coated steel suits small indoor cans. The rose matters more than the body material for how well a can waters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do gardeners turn the watering can rose upward?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fitting a removable rose face up sends water up in a gentle arc so it falls softly, like light rain. That stops seed being washed out of trays and keeps young seedlings upright. Facing the rose forward gives a heavier shower for established plants. It only works on cans with a removable rose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is a full 2 gallon watering can?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The water alone weighs about 16.7 pounds, since a US gallon of water is about 8.3 pounds. Add the can itself and a full 2 gallon can is typically around 18 to 20 pounds. A 2.5 gallon can carries close to 21 pounds of water before you count the steel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What watering can is best for indoor plants?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small can of 0.7 to 1 litre with a long, thin spout, so you can water the soil under the leaves. The Burgon &amp; Ball Indoor can suits crowded shelves, and the Haws Bartley Burbler adds a removable rose for seedlings. Avoid large outdoor cans indoors, which drip and splash.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you leave a watering can outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plastic and galvanized cans can live outside in the growing season, but empty them after use so algae and scale do not build up. Bring them in before freezing nights, since ice splits seams. Powder-coated indoor cans are not made for outdoor storage and may rust at the joints.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
