          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose a watering can by the weight you can carry full, not the biggest capacity on the shelf. For most gardens that means a 1 to 1.5 gallon can with a long spout and a removable brass rose, like the Haws Cradley Deluxe. Add a small long-spout can for houseplants, and keep a cheap plastic can for fertilizer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Look after it and any of these will last for years: empty it after use, store it upside down, keep feed out of galvanized steel and bring it in before the first hard frost. When you find yourself refilling more than five or six times a session, that is the signal to reach for a hose instead.
